<h2>The litigation web</h2>
<p>The parties married in 2009 and have two children, born in 2014 and 2017. After the wife says she was driven from the matrimonial home in July 2020, the filings multiplied: a Section 498A IPC case (charge-sheeted and pending trial), a DV Act complaint, a Section 125 CrPC maintenance case, the husband's criminal complaint alleging misappropriation of Rs 75 lakh by the wife's father, a custody case, and a divorce petition stayed pending the alimony application.</p>
<p>On the interim-maintenance track, the trial court in June 2022 granted the wife Rs 20,000, Rs 15,000 per child, and an accommodation charge of Rs 25,000. In April 2023 the Additional Sessions Judge reworked that: the wife's interim maintenance was set aside, the children's was raised to Rs 25,000 each per month, and arrears were to be paid in five instalments. Both sides came to the High Court.</p>

<h2>What the High Court held</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Contested facts need evidence.</strong> Whether the wife suppressed income, whether she can access her accounts, and the source of substantial funds — including the roughly Rs 1.41 crore the husband says she holds — must be decided on examination and cross-examination, not on rival affidavits at the interim stage.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No perversity in the appellate view.</strong> The ASJ's insistence that both parties disclose actual earnings — and the observation that non-disclosure is akin to defrauding the court — was unimpeachable.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Children's maintenance stands.</strong> Rs 25,000 per child was upheld; school fees alone ran to about Rs 30,000 monthly, and the father's complaint that he was not consulted on admissions did not dilute the children's entitlement.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>All four revisions dismissed.</strong> With a direction that the Section 125 and DV Act Section 12 applications be decided on evidence, preferably within six months, without unnecessary adjournments.</div>
</div>

<h2>Reading the decision with Rajnesh v. Neha</h2>
<p>The only authority pressed was <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em>, (2021) 2 SCC 324, the Supreme Court's framework judgment requiring affidavits of assets and liabilities from both sides in maintenance proceedings. This case illustrates the framework's limits as much as its force: affidavits put each side's financial picture on record, but where the pictures conflict root-and-branch — hidden accounts, routed funds, corporate shareholdings — the interim court takes a provisional view and leaves the truth to trial. The wife's interim award fell at the appellate stage because her own resources were apparent on the record; her final entitlement remains fully open.</p>
