<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<p>For claimants: treat the Rajnesh affidavit as the centrepiece of the case, not a formality — gaps and inconsistencies in disclosure now cost interim relief, not just credibility at trial. For paying spouses: an attack on the other side's disclosure succeeds at the interim stage only to the extent the suppression is apparent from documents already on record; everything else is an argument for expedited evidence, which this judgment's six-month direction exemplifies. And for both: children's maintenance is insulated from the parents' financial warfare — courts will fix it by reference to actual, provable costs such as school fees.</p>
<div class="note">Under the new criminal procedure code, Section 125 CrPC corresponds to Section 144 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023. Pending proceedings instituted under the CrPC continue, and the principles governing interim maintenance and disclosure apply unchanged.</div>
